  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1256 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nA warming trend ensues late weekend into early next as mid-level \nridging and accompanying sinking and drying motion briefly spikes \nahead of a more zonal and progressive upper air pattern. Highs \nrise into the mid to upper 70s and 80s F on Sunday for warmer \nspots over the Upper Trans Pecos into Stockton Plateau and basins \nof Culberson County into the southern Rio Grande basin. The \nweather remains quiet with very low rain chances until Sunday \nnight. By then, a more progressive weather pattern and an \napproaching disturbance bypassing the area to the north results in\nincreased rain chances up to 30% for the northernmost SE NM \nplains. Lows Sunday night then again drop into the upper 50s to \nmid 60s F as southerly winds persist. \n\nA slight cooldown begins early next week as Pacific air moves in \nbehind a surface cold front accompanying passage of the upper air \ndisturbance. In response, winds are expected to shift from \nsoutherly to westerly and bring in cooler air from west to east. \nHowever, the bigger story will be strong winds aloft mixing down \nthe surface. We are monitoring the potential for strong winds at \nleast 35 to 50 mph with higher gusts to develop over the \nGuadalupes/Delawares Monday morning, with 25 to 35 mph winds \nelsewhere. Guidance and grids suggest these winds peaking Monday \nafternoon/evening and continuing through Tuesday. Due to the \nduration, high wind products and wind advisories may need to be \nissued for western higher terrain in subsequent shifts. Highs \ndecrease from the upper 60s to lower 70s F western higher terrain,\nmid 70s to lower 80s F lowlands Monday into the mid 60s to lower \n70s F areawide on Tuesday. Lows likewise fall from the upper 40s \nto mid 50s F Monday night into the lower 40s to lower 50s F by \nTuesday night. Temperatures then moderate slightly by mid to late \nnext week, while rain chances remain very low within the mild but \ndry modified Pacific air mass common leeward of the Rockies during\nthis time of year.",
